The Mercedes-Benz Vito Pure is the entry-level trim for the W447 generation Vito (2014-present), including the 2019 and 2024 updates. It focuses on providing essential features for commercial use.

-   **price_usd**: New prices for a Vito Pure start around £20,630 - £28,602 plus VAT (approx $26,100 - $36,300 USD). Used prices are lower, with a 2020 110 Pure listed around £10,995 - £13,495 + VAT (approx $13,900 - $17,100 USD) and a 2019 114 Pure listed around £8,995 + VAT (approx $11,400 USD). Compared to the average new car price (~$52,000), this is significantly lower. Score: 0.60 (reflecting the lower entry price point).
-   **total_cost_estimate_5yr_usd**: Vans can have varied ownership costs depending on usage and maintenance. Reliability is generally improved in W447, but Mercedes parts can be expensive. Default to average. Score: 0.00.
-   **insurance_usd_yr**: Commercial vehicle insurance rates vary. The 110 Pure is listed with an insurance group of 37. This is relatively high compared to average passenger cars. Score: 0.00.
-   **maint_usd_yr**: Mercedes-Benz parts and labor can be more expensive than mainstream rivals. Score: -0.20.
-   **repair_risk_score**: The Vito has a solid reputation for reliability and consistently appears in the top 10 of the FN50 van reliability survey. Score: 0.70.
-   **hp**: Engine options for the Pure trim include 109 CDI (87-94hp), 110 CDI (101-102hp), and 111 CDI (112-114hp). The 114 CDI (136hp) was also available on Pure initially but seems less common now. Averaging the lower options gives around 100-110 hp. This is very low compared to the overall market average for vehicles today. Score: -1.00.
-   **torque**: Torque figures for the available engines are around 240 Nm (109 CDI), 270 Nm (110 CDI), and 300 Nm (111 CDI). The 114 CDI has 330 Nm. Averaging the lower options gives around 270 Nm (approx 199 lb-ft). This is low compared to the overall market. Score: -0.70.
-   **cylinder_count**: 4-cylinder is average. Score: 0.00.
